#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068614 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "8787186e-94bd-53da-9304-b0d3efbcb966" :question/math-academy-id "e-5823" :question/problem "Solve for $a$ where $\\dfrac{2a -5}{3a^2} + \\dfrac{1}{a} = \\dfrac{a-1}{2a^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $a = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $3a^2,$ $a,$ and $2a^2,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(3a^2, a, 2a^2) = 6a^2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6a^2$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a} & =\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}} \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =6{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot 3{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{3{a}^{2}})+6a\\cdot a\\cdot (\\frac{1}{a}) & =3\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{3{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{2a-5}{\\cancel{3{a}^{2}}})+6a\\cdot \\cancel{a}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{a}}) & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{a}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{a-1}{\\cancel{2{a}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2\\cdot (2a-5)+6a\\cdot (1) & =3\\cdot (a-1) \\\\ 2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $a,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2a-5)+6a & =3(a-1) \\\\ 4a-10+6a & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-10 & =3a-3 \\\\ 10a-3a & =-3+10 \\\\ 7a & =7 \\\\ a & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $a=0$ as a solution. Since $a=1$ is different from $a=0,$ we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $a=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "84d3492d-12bb-50c2-b5b4-5f08567eb4f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5825" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $3 - \\dfrac{10x+7}{8x}  = \\dfrac{6x-3}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $8x$ and $2x,$ so the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(8x,2x) = 8x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $8x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x} & =\\frac{6x-3}{2x} \\\\ 8x\\cdot (3-\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 8x\\cdot 3-8x\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{8x}) & =8x\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{2x}) \\\\ 24x-\\cancel{8x}\\cdot (\\frac{10x+7}{\\cancel{8x}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{6x-3}{\\cancel{2x}}) \\\\ 24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24x-(10x+7) & =4\\cdot (6x-3) \\\\ 14x-7 & =24x-12 \\\\ 24x-14x & =-7+12 \\\\ 10x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x= \\dfrac 12$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x= \\dfrac 12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068629 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "8e0fa2cd-2b29-510a-868f-4779de8536e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-6260" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{x+2}{x} = \\dfrac{x-4}{x} + 2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x = 0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. Both denominators in the equation are $x,$ so the least common denominator is $x.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $x$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2}{x} & =\\frac{x-4}{x}+2 \\\\ x\\cdot (\\frac{x+2}{x}) & =x\\cdot (\\frac{x-4}{x}+2) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{x} & =x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{x}+x\\cdot 2 \\\\ \\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x+2}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{x}}+2x \\\\ x+2 & =x-4+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =x-4+2x \\\\ x+2 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3x-x & =2+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=3$ is different from $x=0,$ we accept it as a solution. \n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "5d20e960-7554-5a05-b1e0-2b98b944e186" :question/math-academy-id "e-6261" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\dfrac{1+x}{x} + \\dfrac{5+x}{6x} = \\dfrac{1-x}{3x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $x = 0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nNow, let's multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator. The equation has the denominators $x, 6x,$ and $3x,$ so the the least common multiple (or $\\textrm{LCM}$) of the denominators is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{LCM}(x, 6x, 3x) = 6x.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation by $6x$ and simplifying, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x} & =\\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x}+\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot \\frac{1-x}{3x} \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =6x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6x\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{x})+6x\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{6x}) & =2\\cdot 3x\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{3x}) \\\\ 6\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1+x}{\\cancel{x}})+\\cancel{6x}\\cdot (\\frac{5+x}{\\cancel{6x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{3x}\\cdot (\\frac{1-x}{\\cancel{3x}}) \\\\ 6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1+x)+(5+x) & =2(1-x) \\\\ 6+6x+5+x & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+11 & =2-2x \\\\ 7x+2x & =2-11 \\\\ 9x & =-9 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=-1$ is different from $x=0,$ we can accept it as the solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce934f1-749e-5c82-9be2-eb4043940d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537322} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb68e51-5ee4-5bd9-86b7-657995c28427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537360} {:answer/id #uuid "6248e345-a77f-5d0b-a6d9-29b049db316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537425} {:answer/id #uuid "b2172526-cf2e-5a91-b2a6-d166613ff60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537574} {:answer/id #uuid "dc07d439-b7d4-549a-97f7-0b5dc5b32343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6248e345-a77f-5d0b-a6d9-29b049db316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537425} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7254ba-8516-55b4-929d-3effa926b080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa991cba-7607-5ad7-99b3-e6c6f2993b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115795" :question/problem "Solve for $q$ where $\\frac{2q-1}{4q}=\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $q=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3q$ and $4q.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3q,4q)=12q.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12q,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1 \\\\ 12q\\cdot (\\frac{2q-1}{4q}) & =12q\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1) \\\\ 12q\\cdot \\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =12q\\cdot \\frac{q+1}{3q}-12q\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{4q}\\cdot (\\frac{2q-1}{\\cancel{4q}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{3q}\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{\\cancel{3q}})-12q \\\\ 3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $q,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =4q+4-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =-8q+4 \\\\ 6q+8q & =4+3 \\\\ 14q & =7 \\\\ q & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $q=0$ as a solution. But since $q=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $q=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c78e8f45-6241-530a-8e86-768b85aa828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c78e8f45-6241-530a-8e86-768b85aa828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537618} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7444c8-c4e7-5947-a2ec-fcf6d2b56070" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21325d3f-ea32-5619-afd8-5e491ef497e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-270354" :question/problem "Solve for $b$ where $\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b}=6.$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $b=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $b,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b} & =6 \\\\ b\\cdot (\\frac{b+5}{b}+\\frac{b-7}{b}) & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ b\\cdot \\frac{b+5}{b}+b\\cdot \\frac{b-7}{b} & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\cancel{b}\\cdot \\frac{b+5}{\\cancel{b}}+\\cancel{b}\\cdot \\frac{b-7}{\\cancel{b}} & =b\\cdot 6 \\\\ b+5+b-7 & =6b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $b,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b+5+b-7 & =6b \\\\ 2b-2 & =6b \\\\ -4b & =2 \\\\ b & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $b=0$ as a solution. However, since $b=-\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $b=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2562c2a5-6799-5040-a6d8-35239d94c6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2562c2a5-6799-5040-a6d8-35239d94c6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293095} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb132b1f-eced-50c2-9b8d-4aadb8c3a8b1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ce28150-db62-5cfe-975f-e10652804e6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-276864" :question/problem "Solve for $f$ where $\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}=\\frac{f+2}{2f}.$\n\n$f=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $f=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $f,\\,2f,$ and $5f.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(f,2f,5f)=10f.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $10f,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =\\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10f(\\frac{f+6}{f}-\\frac{3f+9}{5f}) & =10f(\\frac{f+2}{2f}) \\\\ 10f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-10f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =10f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot f\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{f}-2\\cdot 5f\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{5f} & =5\\cdot 2f\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{2f} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{f}\\cdot \\frac{f+6}{\\cancel{f}}-2\\cdot \\cancel{5f}\\cdot \\frac{3f+9}{\\cancel{5f}} & =5\\cdot \\cancel{2f}\\cdot \\frac{f+2}{\\cancel{2f}} \\\\ 10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $f,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10(f+6)-2(3f+9) & =5(f+2) \\\\ 10f+60-6f-18 & =5f+10 \\\\ 4f+42 & =5f+10 \\\\ -f & =-32 \\\\ f & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $f=0$ as a solution. But since $f=32\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $f=32.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38875fd2-c051-51ae-9cd4-a572e31fc61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38875fd2-c051-51ae-9cd4-a572e31fc61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537344} :answer-field/id #uuid "787aa0da-04c6-5397-99c2-28c45379391c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff75b330-ecd4-5fa0-b36d-564d6c66e864" :question/math-academy-id "q-276869" :question/problem "Solve for $v$ where $\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}=\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}.$\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $v=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $6v,\\,{v}^{2},$ and $2{v}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6v,{v}^{2},2{v}^{2})=6{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{v}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v} & =\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6{v}^{2}(\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}) & =6{v}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6v\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =3\\cdot 2{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{\\cancel{{v}^{2}}}-\\cancel{6v}\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{6v}} & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{2{v}^{2}}} \\\\ 6(v+4)-v\\cdot (7) & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 6(v+4)-7v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $v,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(v+4)-7v & =15 \\\\ 6v+24-7v & =15 \\\\ -v+24 & =15 \\\\ -v & =-9 \\\\ v & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $v=0$ as a solution. But since $v=9\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $v=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "beab791b-0819-5768-9e5f-0dc621d5a462" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Two Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027489bb-28e3-5dfe-a2b6-367b579e0221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537229} {:answer/id #uuid "2ceb2f6f-5d34-5be3-a06c-024dd0503854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537323} {:answer/id #uuid "2e591bdc-0ce1-5740-b9d1-67306bc53ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537327} {:answer/id #uuid "8090692a-b362-571b-91a3-0212386b7f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537479} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8501be-4fcb-577e-8819-117055fdb0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e591bdc-0ce1-5740-b9d1-67306bc53ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537327} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e75216d-bcd6-569a-b621-c249dcbf26bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a581df95-2d51-59ad-a71c-d2138e307e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-3518" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{3-x}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and $3x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,3x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3x,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}) & =3x\\cdot (\\frac{3-x}{3x}) \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} & =3x\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{3x} \\\\ \\cancel{3}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}+3\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{3x}\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{\\cancel{3x}} \\\\ x\\cdot (2)+3\\cdot (1) & =3-x \\\\ 2x+3 & =3-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =3-x \\\\ 2x+x & =3-3 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068880 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5916154-14b6-5e2d-b33c-176b76293927" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing One Distinct Linear Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df39945-f17e-5051-9124-626d55bf8a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537293} {:answer/id #uuid "64742e1b-bec8-5682-b5ee-105f5d4f7a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537429} {:answer/id #uuid "7532c4d0-18e0-53bb-a6b7-c9f7c5d650eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537461} {:answer/id #uuid "abfebc27-8159-524d-9a6e-cdfd59eb0187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537553} {:answer/id #uuid "befbf0c7-7d19-5ffd-943e-139e02963d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df39945-f17e-5051-9124-626d55bf8a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537293} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8e3c1c2-59cd-57ee-a592-7d15bb5c605d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86933fa6-f8f7-5354-b325-40dd23c73f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-75921" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}=\\frac{5x-6}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x} & =\\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}) & =n\\cdot (\\frac{5x-6}{2x}) \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{2x}+2x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{2x} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2x}}+\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{2x}} & =\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{\\cancel{2x}} \\\\ x-2+x-4 & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2+x-4 & =5x-6 \\\\ 2x-6 & =5x-6 \\\\ 5x-2x & =-6+6 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068882 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f63f80b8-f564-56e6-8c90-6918d64e0316" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Three Distinct Linear Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173e1c1a-757c-5fcf-be6a-91e7ca4875db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537278} {:answer/id #uuid "637488d7-0f63-5fe0-8698-1d6414fe8f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537427} {:answer/id #uuid "b33107cc-21e0-55e3-a66c-7183491e915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537576} {:answer/id #uuid "f109c179-6843-5d73-a8d7-5f145c4e38c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537726} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a5d0d-e5eb-5ce1-ad1a-caa3023a2a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f109c179-6843-5d73-a8d7-5f145c4e38c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b11927f-a39d-501b-8f1e-1dbd9842d17d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f095dc29-06a2-59bf-8997-6e524951d317" :question/math-academy-id "q-75936" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}=\\frac{1}{9m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,6m,$ and $9m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,6m,9m)=18m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $18m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m} & =\\frac{1}{9m} \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m})-18m\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 6\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m})-3\\cdot 6m\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =2\\cdot 9m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{\\cancel{3m}})-3\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{\\cancel{6m}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{9m}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{9m}}) \\\\ 6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2 \\\\ 24m+6-3m+3 & =2 \\\\ 21m+9 & =2 \\\\ 21m & =-7 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=-\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537988 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c698912-1f35-5110-8d5d-d490cce2e9a7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Containing Quadratic Denominators"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a2e99-60fa-5845-b485-7f1ecf41d0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537243} {:answer/id #uuid "2db04f8a-deb3-5e11-91d5-eea128cb749d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537326} {:answer/id #uuid "b61f0de4-08b9-5e89-9aaa-a012a63e7f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537581} {:answer/id #uuid "c71bf5dc-237a-5908-9987-a1df036cb19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537615} {:answer/id #uuid "cf71e830-8356-5e8d-bce8-f9b93f8cc125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db04f8a-deb3-5e11-91d5-eea128cb749d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537326} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc35caa-71e9-53b2-85d0-1e3c73992105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7541a79-898f-52f6-9741-385d5ef8e2c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75955" :question/problem "Which value of $n$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3n}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2n,$ ${n}^{2},$ and $3n.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2n,{n}^{2},3n)=6{n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{7}{3n} \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n})-6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot 2n\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n})-6\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =2n\\cdot 3n\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot \\cancel{2n}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2n}})-6\\cdot \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}) & =2n\\cdot \\cancel{3n}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{\\cancel{3n}}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot (3)-6\\cdot (1-n) & =2n\\cdot (7) \\\\ 9n-6(1-n) & =14n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-6(1-n) & =14n \\\\ 15n-6 & =14n \\\\ n & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=6\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=6.$"}]}
